WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:00.970
SAAD ALI: Hello, everyone.

00:00:00.970 --> 00:00:01.670
My name is Saad Ali.

00:00:01.670 --> 00:00:03.030
I'm a software engineer
here at Google,

00:00:03.030 --> 00:00:04.860
and I'm very lucky to
be joined by Kelsey

00:00:04.860 --> 00:00:07.154
Hightower, the greatest
person that I know.

00:00:07.154 --> 00:00:08.570
KELSEY HIGHTOWER:
Ooh, I'm putting

00:00:08.570 --> 00:00:09.850
that on my performance review.

00:00:09.850 --> 00:00:10.320
SAAD ALI: That's right.

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:12.030
Kelsey, can you
tell us what you're

00:00:12.030 --> 00:00:14.406
most excited about at
KubeCon here this year?

00:00:14.406 --> 00:00:15.780
You've been to a
lot of KubeCons.

00:00:15.780 --> 00:00:16.740
KELSEY HIGHTOWER: Yeah.

00:00:16.740 --> 00:00:20.760
We have 8,000 people here,
and what I'm noticing is

00:00:20.760 --> 00:00:23.310
some people still don't
know what Kubernetes is.

00:00:23.310 --> 00:00:25.560
So keeping that
fresh perspective,

00:00:25.560 --> 00:00:28.140
I can learn how we should
be making it better, what

00:00:28.140 --> 00:00:29.640
the real problems are, right?

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:32.070
I haven't had to manage
production infrastructure

00:00:32.070 --> 00:00:33.457
in a few years now.

00:00:33.457 --> 00:00:35.790
So it's kind of nice to know
what the real problems are,

00:00:35.790 --> 00:00:36.900
keeping grounded.

00:00:36.900 --> 00:00:38.190
And just learning from
other people, right?

00:00:38.190 --> 00:00:40.023
So a lot of us considered
us experts, right?

00:00:40.023 --> 00:00:41.962
You're expert in
the storage space.

00:00:41.962 --> 00:00:43.420
But then there's
things-- use cases

00:00:43.420 --> 00:00:44.800
we don't know, so
the opportunity

00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:45.966
to learn from the community.

00:00:45.966 --> 00:00:46.820
SAAD ALI: Yeah.

00:00:46.820 --> 00:00:49.919
So I mean, I've heard a lot
from you about customer empathy,

00:00:49.919 --> 00:00:52.210
not necessarily worrying
about the engineering problem,

00:00:52.210 --> 00:00:55.870
but taking time to
think about, what

00:00:55.870 --> 00:00:58.450
are the problems that we're
actually solving for customers?

00:00:58.450 --> 00:01:00.580
And in that
perspective, you've been

00:01:00.580 --> 00:01:03.220
asking us to take a step
back from Kubernetes

00:01:03.220 --> 00:01:05.230
and look at the problem
more holistically.

00:01:05.230 --> 00:01:06.938
Can you talk about
that and what Google's

00:01:06.938 --> 00:01:08.680
been doing in the
past few quarters

00:01:08.680 --> 00:01:09.910
to actually address that?

00:01:09.910 --> 00:01:10.180
KELSEY HIGHTOWER: Yeah.

00:01:10.180 --> 00:01:12.760
So I think the Kubernetes
team, in particular-- any team

00:01:12.760 --> 00:01:15.550
that's doing engineering
work in the open,

00:01:15.550 --> 00:01:18.130
you start to have empathy for
all the issues that get opened,

00:01:18.130 --> 00:01:18.760
right?

00:01:18.760 --> 00:01:21.820
But for me, I get fortunate that
I get to go see our customers

00:01:21.820 --> 00:01:22.960
and whiteboard.

00:01:22.960 --> 00:01:24.970
And very rarely do we
start with Kubernetes

00:01:24.970 --> 00:01:26.820
as the solution or the problem.

00:01:26.820 --> 00:01:29.230
It's usually we want to do
these five or six things.

00:01:29.230 --> 00:01:31.063
So internally-- you've
been a part of them--

00:01:31.063 --> 00:01:32.290
we do these empathy sessions.

00:01:32.290 --> 00:01:34.060
And the goal is to
take our engineering

00:01:34.060 --> 00:01:37.250
and say, let's feel it the
way our customers feel it.

00:01:37.250 --> 00:01:39.500
And then we actually start
to see that, you know what?

00:01:39.500 --> 00:01:41.110
Without even
mentioning Kubernetes,

00:01:41.110 --> 00:01:43.420
we all have ideas of how
to solve these problems.

00:01:43.420 --> 00:01:45.520
It's bonus points if we
can leverage a platform

00:01:45.520 --> 00:01:47.060
like Kubernetes to solve them.

00:01:47.060 --> 00:01:50.080
So I think it's more of, how can
we make more people successful?

00:01:50.080 --> 00:01:51.886
Sometimes that
involves Kubernetes,

00:01:51.886 --> 00:01:54.010
or sometimes that involves
a new thing, like Istio,

00:01:54.010 --> 00:01:55.510
to solve that kind of problem.

00:01:55.510 --> 00:01:57.460
So that empathy
session is what helps

00:01:57.460 --> 00:02:00.390
me stay grounded and focused
on what the customer needs.

00:02:00.390 --> 00:02:02.140
SAAD ALI: So as you
talk to customers now,

00:02:02.140 --> 00:02:03.515
what are the
problems that you're

00:02:03.515 --> 00:02:05.530
seeing them encounter
that we really need

00:02:05.530 --> 00:02:07.037
to address as a community now?

00:02:07.037 --> 00:02:09.370
KELSEY HIGHTOWER: Honestly,
I think it's the principles,

00:02:09.370 --> 00:02:09.870
right?

00:02:09.870 --> 00:02:12.490
CI/CD has been a good
idea for a long time.

00:02:12.490 --> 00:02:14.560
It isn't just a good idea
because Kubernetes just

00:02:14.560 --> 00:02:15.310
came out.

00:02:15.310 --> 00:02:16.840
So what I try to
ask them is this.

00:02:16.840 --> 00:02:18.520
What is your culture?

00:02:18.520 --> 00:02:20.415
What is your change
management process?

00:02:20.415 --> 00:02:21.790
That's what you're
going to build

00:02:21.790 --> 00:02:23.280
into this concept called CI/CD.

00:02:23.280 --> 00:02:25.150
It's not about Spinnaker
versus Jenkins.

00:02:25.150 --> 00:02:26.470
Pick one.

00:02:26.470 --> 00:02:29.241
Once you pick one, you're going
to articulate your culture

00:02:29.241 --> 00:02:29.740
in that.

00:02:29.740 --> 00:02:32.198
I tell people, you're going to
write a set of batch scripts

00:02:32.198 --> 00:02:34.970
in a specific order, and that's
how you're going to do things.

00:02:34.970 --> 00:02:37.690
If you're using raw VMs,
that will still work.

00:02:37.690 --> 00:02:40.210
When Kubernetes comes in, you
can eliminate a lot of stuff

00:02:40.210 --> 00:02:43.030
that you do in between
CI and Kubernetes,

00:02:43.030 --> 00:02:45.370
and then that's the business
thing that you focus on.

00:02:45.370 --> 00:02:48.160
But then the platform
stays the same.

00:02:48.160 --> 00:02:51.730
Whether you're using Cloud
Functions, Kubernetes,

00:02:51.730 --> 00:02:54.880
or raw VMs, most developers
can just check in code,

00:02:54.880 --> 00:02:56.890
and you hide that complexity.

00:02:56.890 --> 00:02:59.480
That, to me, is the principle
that has never changed.

00:02:59.480 --> 00:03:02.560
I'm telling companies now,
you need to get that done.

00:03:02.560 --> 00:03:04.520
Do not kick that
can down the road.

00:03:04.520 --> 00:03:05.960
Yes, Kubernetes is awesome.

00:03:05.960 --> 00:03:07.550
Has a lot of great technologies.

00:03:07.550 --> 00:03:10.850
But if we just go out and hand
out kubectl out to everyone,

00:03:10.850 --> 00:03:13.600
we're kind of regressing back
to the old ways of doing things.

00:03:13.600 --> 00:03:16.060
Put CI/CD on top, and
just enjoy the fact

00:03:16.060 --> 00:03:19.090
that you're going to have to do
a lot less glue between CI/CD

00:03:19.090 --> 00:03:19.900
and Kubernetes.

00:03:19.900 --> 00:03:24.040
SAAD ALI: So that's great advice
for developers or companies,

00:03:24.040 --> 00:03:25.720
to start focusing
on CI/CD first,

00:03:25.720 --> 00:03:27.601
and let everything
else evolve from there.

00:03:27.601 --> 00:03:30.100
Do you have any other advice
for customers, especially those

00:03:30.100 --> 00:03:31.664
with a lot of
legacy applications,

00:03:31.664 --> 00:03:33.080
and how they should
deal with them

00:03:33.080 --> 00:03:34.652
and think about
this modern world?

00:03:34.652 --> 00:03:35.860
KELSEY HIGHTOWER: It's funny.

00:03:35.860 --> 00:03:37.510
Every customer I meet
and they say "legacy,"

00:03:37.510 --> 00:03:38.770
I say you're not
allowed to say "legacy."

00:03:38.770 --> 00:03:40.234
You have to say "classic."

00:03:40.234 --> 00:03:42.650
Because it's the legacy stuff
that's paying your paycheck,

00:03:42.650 --> 00:03:43.180
right?

00:03:43.180 --> 00:03:44.554
Like, that's where
it comes from.

00:03:44.554 --> 00:03:45.800
This is the tried and true.

00:03:45.800 --> 00:03:47.260
So if you consider
it a classic thing--

00:03:47.260 --> 00:03:47.850
SAAD ALI: An antique.

00:03:47.850 --> 00:03:49.070
KELSEY HIGHTOWER: --then
you kind of appreciate

00:03:49.070 --> 00:03:50.028
it a little bit better.

00:03:50.028 --> 00:03:53.190
So what I tell them is a lot
of the things that are working

00:03:53.190 --> 00:03:55.260
will probably also
work in the new world.

00:03:55.260 --> 00:03:57.400
It's just about what
do you adapt, right?

00:03:57.400 --> 00:04:00.080
So in my keynote, it's funny,
I'm going to be using Fortran,

00:04:00.080 --> 00:04:00.640
right?

00:04:00.640 --> 00:04:03.010
This is one of the original
programming languages.

00:04:03.010 --> 00:04:05.200
Recently had a release in 2018.

00:04:05.200 --> 00:04:07.300
And I'll be using that
to deploy to Kubernetes

00:04:07.300 --> 00:04:08.920
and other serverless
platforms, just

00:04:08.920 --> 00:04:12.010
to show people it's not about
legacy versus new things.

00:04:12.010 --> 00:04:15.520
It's more about we've learned
things in the last 30 years.

00:04:15.520 --> 00:04:18.102
A lot of them are baked
into tools like Kubernetes,

00:04:18.102 --> 00:04:20.560
and here's how you leverage
them with some of your existing

00:04:20.560 --> 00:04:21.060
stuff.

00:04:21.060 --> 00:04:22.630
Sometimes that's a rewrite.

00:04:22.630 --> 00:04:24.129
Sometimes it's a retrofit.

00:04:24.129 --> 00:04:26.170
So I want people to focus
more on the principles.

00:04:26.170 --> 00:04:28.430
Those last forever, even
if the product changes.

00:04:28.430 --> 00:04:29.680
SAAD ALI: And do they need to?

00:04:29.680 --> 00:04:31.060
Do they need to
modernize at all,

00:04:31.060 --> 00:04:33.430
or is that an option, to
stay put where they are?

00:04:33.430 --> 00:04:35.680
KELSEY HIGHTOWER: So when I
think about modernization,

00:04:35.680 --> 00:04:38.140
if you have an app
that you've neglected--

00:04:38.140 --> 00:04:40.240
you've not updated it,
you haven't patched it,

00:04:40.240 --> 00:04:43.870
you're using outdated software
that hasn't any more support--

00:04:43.870 --> 00:04:44.620
you're vulnerable.

00:04:44.620 --> 00:04:46.930
There are security reasons
why you need to update that.

00:04:46.930 --> 00:04:48.940
It's not even about
modernization.

00:04:48.940 --> 00:04:50.570
You need to stay on top of it.

00:04:50.570 --> 00:04:53.080
So software is a liability.

00:04:53.080 --> 00:04:56.050
Once you write some
software, it's yours.

00:04:56.050 --> 00:04:57.820
And you got to
nurture and feed it.

00:04:57.820 --> 00:04:59.679
So whether you decide
to leapfrog and say,

00:04:59.679 --> 00:05:01.720
you know what, if we're
going to make adjustments

00:05:01.720 --> 00:05:04.380
to this to improve
security posture,

00:05:04.380 --> 00:05:06.220
maybe we adapt
containers, and that gets

00:05:06.220 --> 00:05:07.670
us there a little bit quicker.

00:05:07.670 --> 00:05:09.220
So to me, the
modernization is more

00:05:09.220 --> 00:05:11.260
about there are new
things available that

00:05:11.260 --> 00:05:13.480
were not available
before, and how are we

00:05:13.480 --> 00:05:14.507
going to leverage them?

00:05:14.507 --> 00:05:16.090
And then label that
whatever you want.

00:05:16.090 --> 00:05:19.540
But to me, you still need to do
that, regardless, for security,

00:05:19.540 --> 00:05:21.162
just so you know how it works.

00:05:21.162 --> 00:05:22.870
But yeah, leverage
some of the new stuff.

00:05:22.870 --> 00:05:23.994
SAAD ALI: That makes sense.

00:05:23.994 --> 00:05:26.830
So security is a very, very
good reason to not stay

00:05:26.830 --> 00:05:29.784
put because you will become
a target very quickly.

00:05:29.784 --> 00:05:30.700
KELSEY HIGHTOWER: Yes.

00:05:30.700 --> 00:05:30.940
SAAD ALI: OK.

00:05:30.940 --> 00:05:31.648
That makes sense.

00:05:31.648 --> 00:05:33.790
So looking forward
into 2019, what

00:05:33.790 --> 00:05:35.555
are you most excited about?

00:05:35.555 --> 00:05:37.930
KELSEY HIGHTOWER: I think
2019, we're starting to settle.

00:05:37.930 --> 00:05:40.013
I've been looking at the
Kubernetes release notes.

00:05:40.013 --> 00:05:41.775
Not a lot of big
bang new features.

00:05:41.775 --> 00:05:42.400
SAAD ALI: Yeah.

00:05:42.400 --> 00:05:44.441
KELSEY HIGHTOWER: So we're
starting to stabilize.

00:05:44.441 --> 00:05:46.440
And as we stabilize,
in 2019, I think

00:05:46.440 --> 00:05:49.110
we have more people
saying the same things.

00:05:49.110 --> 00:05:50.640
Everyone knows what
a container is.

00:05:50.640 --> 00:05:52.080
You know what a pod is.

00:05:52.080 --> 00:05:54.090
We're kind of starting
to understand networking.

00:05:54.090 --> 00:05:55.140
But we're also
starting to understand

00:05:55.140 --> 00:05:56.190
where the value line is.

00:05:56.190 --> 00:05:58.270
This is where Kubernetes stops.

00:05:58.270 --> 00:06:00.000
This is where Istio takes over.

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:01.739
Here's where CI/CD plays.

00:06:01.739 --> 00:06:03.030
Here's where serverless starts.

00:06:03.030 --> 00:06:04.446
Like, Native has
given people kind

00:06:04.446 --> 00:06:07.500
of this clear line of starting
to think about this functions

00:06:07.500 --> 00:06:08.940
as a service kind of thing.

00:06:08.940 --> 00:06:11.950
So I think 2019, we're
going to start to, now,

00:06:11.950 --> 00:06:14.730
get a return on value from this
big open source investment,

00:06:14.730 --> 00:06:15.229
right?

00:06:15.229 --> 00:06:17.030
You've been working
on Kubernetes forever.

00:06:17.030 --> 00:06:19.070
I've been involved
for a very long time.

00:06:19.070 --> 00:06:19.810
And when you start
to look at it,

00:06:19.810 --> 00:06:21.470
it's like, when do we start
getting a payback on this?

00:06:21.470 --> 00:06:23.920
So now people using GKE
don't even think about it.

00:06:23.920 --> 00:06:25.520
Oh, there's a security
vulnerability.

00:06:25.520 --> 00:06:25.840
Click.

00:06:25.840 --> 00:06:26.980
We've upgraded it for them.

00:06:26.980 --> 00:06:27.550
SAAD ALI: That's right.

00:06:27.550 --> 00:06:29.799
KELSEY HIGHTOWER: So now,
we're starting to see return

00:06:29.799 --> 00:06:30.820
on investment in 2019.

00:06:30.820 --> 00:06:32.236
And now, we're
going to also start

00:06:32.236 --> 00:06:33.850
to identify what's
missing, right?

00:06:33.850 --> 00:06:35.920
We know what we can do
in Kubernetes to improve,

00:06:35.920 --> 00:06:39.700
but there's a whole layer
between just run my code

00:06:39.700 --> 00:06:41.650
and what Kubernetes does.

00:06:41.650 --> 00:06:42.940
That's 2019.

00:06:42.940 --> 00:06:44.860
SAAD ALI: That sounds wonderful.

00:06:44.860 --> 00:06:47.714
So just to wrap things up,
let's talk about KubeCon.

00:06:47.714 --> 00:06:49.630
I'm going to put you on
the spot a little bit.

00:06:49.630 --> 00:06:51.921
What's your favorite KubeCon
memory of all the KubeCons

00:06:51.921 --> 00:06:52.917
that you've been to?

00:06:52.917 --> 00:06:54.250
KELSEY HIGHTOWER: You know what?

00:06:54.250 --> 00:06:57.205
The very first KubeCon
was purely community.

00:06:57.205 --> 00:06:57.830
SAAD ALI: Yeah.

00:06:57.830 --> 00:06:59.200
KELSEY HIGHTOWER:
There was no CNCF yet.

00:06:59.200 --> 00:07:00.100
There was no big sponsors yet.

00:07:00.100 --> 00:07:01.400
SAAD ALI: This was
in San Francisco.

00:07:01.400 --> 00:07:01.510
KELSEY HIGHTOWER: Yeah.

00:07:01.510 --> 00:07:04.060
It was just a bunch of
people that got in one spot,

00:07:04.060 --> 00:07:05.530
and we were all betting.

00:07:05.530 --> 00:07:07.660
It wasn't the
number one platform.

00:07:07.660 --> 00:07:10.000
We were still like, is
Docker Swarm the way to go?

00:07:10.000 --> 00:07:11.432
Is Mesos the way to go?

00:07:11.432 --> 00:07:12.890
So we got in that
room and we said,

00:07:12.890 --> 00:07:14.171
hey, here's what we're doing.

00:07:14.171 --> 00:07:15.170
Here's how we're trying.

00:07:15.170 --> 00:07:17.628
And I remember, I think Michelle
Noorali posted the Twitter

00:07:17.628 --> 00:07:19.180
thing, how many
people are running

00:07:19.180 --> 00:07:20.670
Kubernetes on production?

00:07:20.670 --> 00:07:22.820
And we had them all get
on the stage, right?

00:07:22.820 --> 00:07:26.110
And all of them could probably
fit right here if we tried to.

00:07:26.110 --> 00:07:29.200
And that was a very
authentic conference, right?

00:07:29.200 --> 00:07:31.522
It was like everyone--
you had to buy in.

00:07:31.522 --> 00:07:32.980
And now, when you
zoom forward, you

00:07:32.980 --> 00:07:35.270
can kind of see those
people are still here.

00:07:35.270 --> 00:07:37.486
So we knew that we built
a very strong community

00:07:37.486 --> 00:07:38.860
in the very
beginning, and that's

00:07:38.860 --> 00:07:40.907
why I can never forget that.

00:07:40.907 --> 00:07:43.240
SAAD ALI: I think one of the
big reasons that Kubernetes

00:07:43.240 --> 00:07:45.616
is the scale that
it is now is because

00:07:45.616 --> 00:07:46.990
of the community
that it nurtured

00:07:46.990 --> 00:07:50.597
and the leadership that came
from the bottom up, basically.

00:07:50.597 --> 00:07:52.430
And I think you played
a large part of that.

00:07:52.430 --> 00:07:52.960
Thank you, Kelsey.

00:07:52.960 --> 00:07:53.710
KELSEY HIGHTOWER:
Awesome, thank you.

00:07:53.710 --> 00:07:54.100
SAAD ALI: All right.

00:07:54.100 --> 00:07:54.520
Take care.

00:07:54.520 --> 00:07:55.436
KELSEY HIGHTOWER: Bye.

00:07:55.436 --> 00:07:57.388
[MUSIC PLAYING]

